            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Suspension Spike: Violation Breakdown</h2>
<p>According to Yiyu Digital Media's monitoring system, 17 Google Ads accounts requested appeal assistance this week. 8 were suspended for <strong>landing page issues</strong>, 5 for <strong>conversion tracking anomalies</strong>, and the rest for policy violations.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Landing page issues remain the top cause, but a new trend emerged: Google tightened reviews on privacy policy pages for cross-border e-commerce sites. Three clients had their "About Us" and "Return Policy" pages flagged as "insufficient information."</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>This Week's Top Violation List:</strong><br />
- Landing pages not loading or redirecting abnormally (47%)<br />
- Missing clear refund policies on checkout pages (29%)<br />
- Mismatch between GTM tracking code and Google Ads account (18%)<br />
- Promoting brand keywords or using competitor trademarks (6%)</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Real Case: 72-Hour Unban for a Utility App</h2>
<p>A utility app team discovered their Google Ads account suspended on Tuesday afternoon, citing "low landing page experience score." Here's their appeal preparation process:</p>
<p><strong>Day 1:</strong> Screenshotted the suspension notice and saved violation details, clearly noting the violation ID.</p>
<p><strong>Day 2:</strong> The tech team fixed the landing page—optimized loading speed, added HTTPS certificate, updated privacy policy. After fixes, they recorded a screen video demonstrating the page functions normally.</p>
<p><strong>Day 2 Evening:</strong> Drafted the appeal letter with this structure:<br />
- Acknowledge the issue<br />
- List completed fixes (with specific URLs)<br />
- Commit to future compliance<br />
- Request re-review</p>
<p><strong>Result:</strong> Unbanned Friday morning, approximately 72-hour review cycle.</p>
<p>The key wasn't luck—it was <strong>completeness of evidence</strong>. Screenshots, videos, and specific links were all essential.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Common Appeal Mistakes</h2>
<p><strong>Mistake 1: Only stating "we'll comply going forward"</strong></p>
<p>Reviewers handle hundreds of appeals daily. Promises without evidence won't convince them. Fixes must be documented.</p>
<p><strong>Mistake 2: Ignoring format requirements</strong></p>
<p>Google Ads appeal forms have attachment restrictions. PDF and images are safest—avoid compressed files or cloud links.</p>
<p><strong>Mistake 3: Submitting multiple appeals simultaneously</strong></p>
<p>Duplicate submissions get flagged as "harassment," which actually extends review time. Wait 5 business days before a second attempt.</p>
<hr />
<h2>This Week's Recommendations</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Backup account settings in advance:</strong> Two clients this week couldn't provide historical performance data when requested during appeals. Without exported records, they were left in a difficult position.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Build a landing page checklist:</strong> Before launching new accounts, verify compliance against the Google Ads policy page. Focus on mobile loading speed, payment transparency, and return policy accessibility.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>The golden appeal template:</strong> Violation screenshot + fix screenshot + page video + commitment letter. This four-part package covers most landing page-related suspensions.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><strong>Trend Outlook:</strong> Google's policy tightening on cross-border e-commerce won't stop here. Teams should complete a full-site policy audit before next week, especially for landing pages handling EU user data—GDPR compliance statements need standalone pages.</p>
